WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.318
This paper, presented at ICML 2024, introduces the
Distance Aware Bottleneck,

00:00:05.398 --> 00:00:10.852
a new way to give deep neural networks the sense of
"knowing what they don't know." The

00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:16.641
authors reframe uncertainty quantification as a
rate-distortion problem: the network learns

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:20.012
a compact codebook that summarizes its training data,

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:25.737
and the distance of a new input from that codebook
becomes a principled uncertainty score,

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.900
computed in a single forward pass.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:33.025
A trustworthy model should know when it is
operating far from what it has seen.

00:00:33.105 --> 00:00:34.857
Deep neural networks, however,

00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:40.903
often make confident predictions even on inputs
that are wildly different from their training data.

00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:47.865
Classical probabilistic models such as Gaussian
Processes have a built-in sense of distance from the training set,

00:00:47.945 --> 00:00:50.796
but standard deep networks do not, and reliable,

00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:55.560
efficient uncertainty estimation for real
deployments remains an open problem.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.105
Today's fast,

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:04.350
single-forward-pass uncertainty methods usually
depend on special architectural tricks,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.595
like spectral normalization, to stop the network's
features from collapsing.

00:01:08.675 --> 00:01:12.840
Those constraints can quietly damage calibration
and are awkward to bolt onto large pre-trained models.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.085
The alternative, deep ensembles and Bayesian networks,

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:21.330
are naturally distance-aware but require many
forward passes,

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:25.575
which is expensive at scale.

00:01:25.655 --> 00:01:29.820
The authors ask whether a single deterministic
model can be distance-aware without these drawbacks.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:36.093
Their key idea is to view uncertainty
quantification through the lens of rate-distortion theory.

00:01:36.173 --> 00:01:41.506
They compress the entire training set into a small
codebook of prototype distributions,

00:01:41.586 --> 00:01:44.865
and measure how far a new input is from that codebook.

00:01:44.945 --> 00:01:47.416
This gives the Distance Aware Bottleneck,

00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:52.580
or DAB: a single deterministic model that produces
uncertainty in one forward pass.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.931
Along the way they contribute a practical
alternating-minimization training algorithm,

00:01:58.011 --> 00:02:02.846
a meta-probabilistic distortion that operates over
distributions of embeddings,

00:02:02.926 --> 00:02:08.570
and a post-hoc variant that adds distance awareness
to large pre-trained feature extractors.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.075
DAB builds on the Information Bottleneck framework
but replaces its rate term with an

00:02:13.155 --> 00:02:17.279
achievable rate borrowed from rate-distortion
theory with finite cardinality.

00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:21.484
Concretely,

00:02:21.564 --> 00:02:25.688
the model learns a codebook of centroid
distributions that quantize the encoders of all training points.

00:02:25.768 --> 00:02:29.893
The uncertainty of a new example is simply its
expected statistical distance,

00:02:29.973 --> 00:02:34.097
here the Kullback-Leibler divergence, from that codebook.

00:02:34.177 --> 00:02:38.302
Training alternates between gradient updates of the
encoder and decoder and cheap analytic

00:02:38.382 --> 00:02:42.506
updates of the soft assignments and centroids,

00:02:42.586 --> 00:02:46.711
echoing the classic Blahut-Arimoto algorithm.

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:50.915
The result is closely analogous to a Gaussian Process,

00:02:50.995 --> 00:02:55.120
where the codebook plays the role of inducing
points and statistical distance replaces Euclidean distance.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.921
The method is tested across several settings.

00:03:00.001 --> 00:03:04.422
On CIFAR-10 as the in-distribution data,

00:03:04.502 --> 00:03:08.923
SVHN serves as a far out-of-distribution set and
CIFAR-100 as a harder near out-of-distribution set.

00:03:09.003 --> 00:03:13.424
The authors also study misclassification prediction
on CIFAR-10 and scale up to ImageNet-1K

00:03:13.504 --> 00:03:17.926
with ImageNet-O as the out-of-distribution set.

00:03:18.006 --> 00:03:22.427
Notably,

00:03:22.507 --> 00:03:26.928
DAB works with a very narrow eight-dimensional
latent bottleneck and just ten distributional codes,

00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:31.429
and is compared against strong baselines including
deep ensembles, DDU, DUQ, DUE,

00:03:31.509 --> 00:03:35.930
SNGP, and the vanilla variational Information Bottleneck.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:41.261
The headline result is that DAB outperforms every
baseline on both out-of-distribution tasks.

00:03:41.341 --> 00:03:46.292
Trained on CIFAR-10, it reaches an AUROC of 0.986
and AUPRC of 0.994 against SVHN,

00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:51.324
and an AUROC of 0.922 and AUPRC of 0.915 against
the harder CIFAR-100,

00:03:51.404 --> 00:03:56.355
beating even a five-model deep ensemble.

00:03:56.435 --> 00:04:01.386
Crucially,

00:04:01.466 --> 00:04:06.417
it does this in a single forward pass with about
thirty-six and a half million parameters,

00:04:06.498 --> 00:04:11.449
versus the ensemble's roughly one hundred and
eighty-two million,

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:16.480
while keeping accuracy on par at about ninety-six percent.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.191
On the task of predicting its own mistakes on CIFAR-10,

00:04:21.271 --> 00:04:24.479
DAB reaches a calibration AUROC of 0.930.

00:04:24.559 --> 00:04:28.889
That nearly closes the gap to a deep ensemble at 0.951,

00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:36.428
while dramatically outperforming other single-pass
deterministic methods such as DDU at 0.632,

00:04:36.508 --> 00:04:40.117
DUE at 0.856, DUQ at 0.889, and SNGP at 0.897.

00:04:40.197 --> 00:04:47.575
A visualization of the learned codebook further
shows each of the ten centroids progressively

00:04:47.655 --> 00:04:52.547
attracting test points of a single class as
training proceeds,

00:04:52.627 --> 00:04:57.920
confirming that the codes capture meaningful
structure in the data.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.539
To summarize the numbers: on CIFAR-10,

00:05:01.619 --> 00:05:09.314
DAB achieves a best-in-class OOD AUROC of 0.986
against SVHN and 0.922 against CIFAR-100,

00:05:09.394 --> 00:05:13.769
and a misclassification calibration AUROC of 0.930.

00:05:13.849 --> 00:05:15.341
At ImageNet scale,

00:05:15.421 --> 00:05:22.766
DAB built on a fine-tuned ResNet-50 beats a
five-model ensemble on misclassification,

00:05:22.846 --> 00:05:29.405
0.868 versus 0.861, and on out-of-distribution
detection against ImageNet-O,

00:05:29.485 --> 00:05:35.257
0.743 versus 0.642, all while using far fewer
trainable parameters,

00:05:35.337 --> 00:05:41.110
roughly thirty-six million versus one hundred and
eighteen million.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.587
The takeaway is that recasting uncertainty as the
problem of compressing training data

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:51.884
into a learned codebook gives a single
deterministic network a genuine sense of distance

00:05:51.964 --> 00:05:53.208
from what it has seen,

00:05:53.288 --> 00:05:57.482
letting it match or beat expensive ensembles at a
fraction of the cost.

00:05:57.562 --> 00:06:01.575
Because the notion of distance is statistical
rather than geometric,

00:06:01.655 --> 00:06:02.839
DAB offers a unified,

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:08.377
Gaussian-Process-like view of uncertainty that
works for both classification and regression,

00:06:08.457 --> 00:06:12.470
and can even be attached after the fact to large
pre-trained models.
